Migrants
Refugees
Children",,"United Kingdom
Ireland","The Group’s account opening process uses a well‑established, risk‑based customer screening approach. Customer due diligence (CDD) assesses money laundering risk and applies enhanced ratings to higher‑risk business customers, including those with potential Modern Slavery exposure due to their operations or supply chains. Front-line staff are trained to identify vulnerable customers, such as those lacking control over documents or acting under duress, with support available where concerns arise.","In 2025 AIB continued to focus on our Supplier risk, in relation to both new engagements and renewal of contract.",Within your supply chains,"Tier 1 suppliers
Tier 2 suppliers
Tier 3 suppliers and below","Women
Migrants
Refugees
Children",,"United Kingdom
Ireland","Our supplier due diligence reflects the nature, value, complexity, and criticality of services procured. We use market intelligence, defined selection criteria, and best‑in‑class tools to identify suitable suppliers. High‑value or high‑risk services undergo enhanced due diligence on the supplier and service model, while lower‑risk or lower‑value suppliers are subject to standard financial and sanctions checks.",,,,,,,,,Yes,,,"In 2025, we strengthened measures to prevent Modern Slavery across our value chain. We introduced an enhanced Whistleblowing Policy with an anonymous external portal and advocacy network, published a Supplier Sustainability Handbook, and reinforced our Code through training and attestations. We delivered 66,028 hours of vulnerability training, achieved an A rating in CDP's Supplier Engagement Assessment 2025 and improved payment practices, with reduced average payment times of supplier invoices.",2026-06-18T16:14:37,https://downloads.modern-slavery-statement-registry.service.gov.uk/pdf-published/DEG2BgVW/2026/modern-slavery-statement-2026.pdf
