Tier 2 suppliers",Women,,Kenya,"We require growers in higher-risk countries to hold credible social certifications from the FSI basket of standards and track certification status monthly. In 2025, 86.46% of our flowers came from fully FSI-certified growers and over 95% of our Kenyan flowers from Fairtrade-certified farms. Growers sign our Supplier Code of Conduct. Our COO has visited our main Kenyan growers, and we stay in close contact with them about their recruitment and employment practices.",Agency labour: seasonal agency workers in our warehouses in the UK (Nuneaton) and the Netherlands (Amstelveen) face a higher risk of exploitation during intense peak periods.,Within your own operations,,"Women
Migrants",,"United Kingdom
Netherlands","We only use licensed, reputable agencies (GLAA in the UK, SNA in the Netherlands) and verify them before placement. Every year after peak we audit our agencies, checking right-to-work records, a sample of payslips against what we paid, and worker handbooks. All seven key UK and NL agencies passed; we are following up improvement points with one. Workers receive modern slavery training and can report concerns anonymously through our Speak Up channels.",Cacao/chocolate supply chains: cocoa farmers in sourcing countries such as Ivory Coast and Ghana face sector-wide risks of forced and child labour.,Within your supply chains,"Tier 2 suppliers
Tier 3 suppliers and below","Women
Migrants
Children",,"Ivory Coast
Ghana","We assess chocolate suppliers and their supply chains, reviewing policies, audits and certifications, and prioritise partners with Fairtrade, Rainforest Alliance or Tony’s Open Chain credentials. In 2026 we ran a formal tender for our own-label chocolate, scoring suppliers on child labour monitoring and remediation, forced labour, living income, cocoa traceability and deforestation, and selected our partners on that basis.",,Yes,,,"For the first time our statement sets KPIs we will report on every year: flowers from FSI-certified growers (86.46%), Kenyan flowers from Fairtrade farms (over 95%), new supplier contracts with our minimum labour principles (100%), colleagues completing modern slavery and due diligence training, and agency audits (all seven key agencies passed). Next year we will finalise our remediation protocol, add modern slavery to our Nuneaton induction and follow up audit findings.",2026-10-14T06:43:57,https://downloads.modern-slavery-statement-registry.service.gov.uk/pdf-published/AETgjriL/2026/Modern Slavery Statement 2026 (signed).pdf
